<span class="kicker">2026-08-09 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Build a Paver Walkway With Materials From Home Depot</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>If you’re planning to source materials from a big box store like Home Depot, the good news is that everything you need for a paver walkway is readily available there, pavers, base gravel, sand, edge restraints, and even tool rental for the equipment you don’t want to buy outright. </p>
<p>The process itself doesn’t change based on where you buy materials, but knowing what to look for in each product category makes the shopping trip a lot more efficient than wandering the aisles hoping to recognize what you need.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we build paver walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and we get questions fairly often from homeowners planning a DIY project about what to actually buy and where. This guide walks through the full building process with an eye toward what’s typically available at a store like Home Depot, so you know what to look for once you’re there.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Find in the Paver and Hardscape Aisle</b></h2>
<h3><b>Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Home Depot carries a range of concrete pavers in various sizes, colors, and finishes, along with some brick and natural stone options depending on your local store’s inventory. Availability varies by location and season, so it’s worth calling ahead or checking online inventory before making the trip, especially if you need a specific quantity for a larger walkway.</p>
<h3><b>Base Gravel and Road Base</b></h3>
<p>Crushed gravel or road base material, sold in bags or in bulk depending on the store, is what your compacted base layer is built from. For anything beyond a very small project, bulk delivery is usually more practical and cost-effective than buying and hauling individual bags yourself.</p>
<h3><b>Sand</b></h3>
<p>Both coarse bedding sand and polymeric jointing sand are typically stocked separately, since they serve different purposes. Bedding sand creates the level surface pavers sit on, while polymeric sand fills the joints and hardens once activated with water. Don’t substitute one for the other, they’re formulated differently for their specific jobs.</p>
<h3><b>Edge Restraints and Landscape Fabric</b></h3>
<p>Plastic or metal edge restraints, along with the spikes used to anchor them, are generally stocked near the paver section. Landscape fabric is usually found in the same general area or in the broader landscaping aisle.</p>
<h2><b>Renting Tools Instead of Buying Them</b></h2>
<h3><b>Plate Compactor Rental</b></h3>
<p>A plate compactor is essential for properly compacting your base and final walkway, but it’s not a tool most homeowners need often enough to justify buying. Home Depot’s tool rental program typically offers plate compactors by the day or the half day, which is usually more than enough time for a standard residential walkway project. Our guide on<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> choosing the right plate compactor size</a> covers what size is appropriate for a walkway-scale project versus a larger patio or driveway.</p>
<h3><b>Other Tools Worth Renting Versus Buying</b></h3>
<p>A paver saw is another tool worth considering for rental rather than purchase if this is a one-time project, since a quality wet saw for masonry cutting represents a real investment that may not make sense for a single walkway. Hand tools like a rubber mallet, level, and shovel are generally worth owning outright, since they’re inexpensive and useful for other projects down the line.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Need: Full Materials and Tools List</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials to purchase</b></p>
<ul><li>Pavers, plus 5 to 10 percent extra for cuts and waste</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, quantity based on your excavated square footage and depth</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Edge restraints and spikes</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools to rent</b></p>
<ul><li>Plate compactor</li><li>Paver saw, if not comfortable using an angle grinder for cuts</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools to own</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>Wheelbarrow</li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>

