<h2><b>Step by Step: Building Your Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Plan and Measure</b></h3>
<p>Before shopping, measure your intended walkway’s length and width to calculate total square footage. This determines how much of each material you need and prevents an underestimated trip that requires a return visit mid-project.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Mark Your Layout</b></h3>
<p>Use stakes and string, or a garden hose, to mark the exact path and width of your walkway on site.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Excavate the Path</b></h3>
<p>Dig out the marked area to a depth accounting for base, sand, and paver thickness combined, generally 7 to 9 inches total for a standard walkway.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Add and Compact the Base</b></h3>
<p>Fill the excavated area with your gravel base material in 2 inch layers, compacting each layer with your rented plate compactor before adding the next.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Lay Landscape Fabric</b></h3>
<p>Roll landscape fabric over the compacted base to control weeds without blocking drainage.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Add and Screed the Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Spread about 1 inch of coarse sand over the fabric, using screed rails to create a level surface for the pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Lay the Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Place pavers following your chosen pattern, starting from a fixed edge and tapping each into position with a rubber mallet. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> covers pattern options and technique in more depth.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Cut Border Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Measure and cut border pieces individually to fit the remaining gaps once the main field is laid.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Install Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Secure edge restraints along both sides of the walkway, anchoring with spikes to lock the pattern in place.</p>
<h3><b>Step 10: Fill Joints and Final Compact</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints, mist to activate, and run the plate compactor over the finished walkway one final time before returning it to the rental counter.</p>
<h2><b>Budgeting Your Trip</b></h2>
<p>Since specific pricing changes regularly and varies by location, it’s worth checking current pricing online or calling ahead for your local store before finalizing a budget. As a general guide, our<a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-paver-walkway-cost/"> paver walkway cost guide</a> breaks down typical per square foot material costs for a DIY project, which should translate reasonably well to big box store pricing, though exact numbers will vary.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes to Avoid When Sourcing From a Big Box Store</b></h2>
<ul><li>Underestimating quantity and needing a second trip mid-project</li><li>Buying plain sand instead of polymeric sand for the joints, which won’t harden or resist weeds the same way</li><li>Skipping tool rental and trying to compact the base by hand, which won’t achieve the same density</li><li>Not checking paver availability and batch consistency before buying, since colors can vary between production runs</li><li>Forgetting edge restraints and spikes, which are easy to overlook on a first shopping trip</li></ul>
<h2><b>When a DIY Project Might Still Be Worth Handing to a Professional</b></h2>
<p>Sourcing your own materials and renting equipment is a completely reasonable way to keep costs down on a straightforward, flat, relatively short walkway. That said, certain situations still tip the scales toward hiring a professional, a sloped site, a longer or curved walkway, heavy clay soil requiring extra base depth, or simply not having the time or physical ability to handle a multi-day project involving heavy material moving and compaction work.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of building paver walkways across the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design can help with either the full installation or just a consultation if you want a second opinion before starting a DIY project. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see the kind of work we build when the project calls for professional installation.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Can I get everything I need for a paver walkway at Home Depot?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, pavers, base gravel, sand, edge restraints, and tool rental for equipment like a plate compactor are typically all available, though specific product availability varies by location.</p>
<h3><b>Should I rent or buy a plate compactor?</b></h3>
<p>For a single walkway project, renting is generally more cost-effective than buying, since a plate compactor isn’t a tool most homeowners need regularly.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the difference between bedding sand and polymeric sand?</b></h3>
<p>Bedding sand creates the level surface pavers sit on, while polymeric sand fills the joints between pavers and hardens once activated with water, helping resist weeds and joint erosion.</p>
<h3><b>How much extra material should I buy for cuts and waste?</b></h3>
<p>Budget for 5 to 10 percent extra pavers beyond your measured square footage to account for cuts, particularly around edges and borders.</p>
<h3><b>Is it cheaper to buy gravel in bags or bulk?</b></h3>
<p>For anything beyond a very small project, bulk delivery is usually more cost-effective and far less labor-intensive than buying and hauling individual bags.</p>
<h3><b>Do I need any special tools beyond what’s mentioned here?</b></h3>
<p>The list above covers what’s needed for a standard walkway. More complex projects, like curved layouts or intricate patterns, may benefit from a wet saw for cleaner cuts.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Start Your Paver Walkway Project</b></h2>
<p>Whether you’re sourcing everything yourself or want a professional opinion before starting, having the right materials and tools lined up from the start makes the whole project go more smoothly.</p>
